// Real case: HBV cccDNA excision
// Multiple groups (Seeger & Sohn 2014, Ramanan et al. 2015, Stone et al. 2021)
//
// Mechanism: CRISPR-Cas9 targeting of HBV cccDNA (covalently closed circular DNA)
// that persists in hepatocyte nuclei as the template for chronic infection.
// Dual guides flanking the HBV genome for excision.
//
// Published outcome (Stone et al., Molecular Therapy 2021):
//   - AAV-delivered Cas9 + dual guides in HBV mouse models
//   - Significant reduction in cccDNA and HBsAg
//   - Complete cccDNA elimination NOT achieved
//   - Hepatocyte turnover complicates long-term assessment
//
// Known ground truth: eliminates_reservoir should be REFUSED.
// cccDNA is remarkably stable, and no published study has demonstrated
// complete elimination of the HBV reservoir. The exhaustive claim
// (hepatocyte-only residence) is also questionable — HBV DNA has been
// detected in PBMCs and other extrahepatic sites.

target Hbv_CccDNA {
  organism human
  tolerance 0
  signatures all [ ACCGTAGCTAACGATCG, TGCAATCGGTACCTGAA ]
  in [ hepatocyte ] @asserted(human)
}

excise Hbv_CccDNA
